omplete: \"Mark story complete ONLY after code-reviewer returns PASS.\"\n\nHmm, but wait. Actually, let me re-read: \"MUST spawn and run planner + code-reviewer every loop.\" OK, spawn both to be safe? Spawning a planner for a completed scaffold story is… the planner will look at the story and the committed state and probably return \"work is complete, no plan needed.\" That's fine, cheap enough.\n\nActually, let me think about what's really being asked here. This is iteration N+1 after failure. The previous iteration did: planner (COMPLETE) → implement (committed 6cc5d66) → code-reviewer (APPROVE) → then marker+commit got killed. The harness deleted the marker and re-ran me. The retry context says: build on top, don't redo, focus on what's incomplete. What's incomplete is: marker + commit + push.\n\nI think the cleanest, most defensible action is:\n1. Verify state myself (read-only): tests pass, package.json matches AC, git state is good. (Orchestrator explicitly allowed to verify.)\n2. Spawn a code-reviewer sub-agent for a final review of the current committed state (respecting the loop contract; cheap; and \"mark story complete ONLY after code-reviewer returns PASS\" — I want a PASS on record for this iteration).\n3. On PASS: recreate marker, commit (small, targeted add of the marker + housekeeping changes), push with timeout, verify.\n4. Report.\n\nWait, but do I really need to wait for the code-reviewer? Yes: \"Mark story complete ONLY after code-reviewer returns PASS.\"\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether to spawn the planner too. \"MUST spawn and run planner + code-reviewer every loop.\" Ugh. OK — I'll spawn both. The planner's prompt: review the story and current state, confirm the implementation plan is satisfied / identify any remaining work. Should be fast. And the code reviewer will review the diff.\n\nActually, hmm, one more consideration: sub-agents run asynchronously by default. I can spawn both in parallel (they're independent). Then block on each via 